{"name":"Familiar Beige","hex":"#cab3a0","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"familiar","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Known to one, or generally known; commonplace.","Acquainted."]},{"part":"Noun","definitions":["An attendant spirit, often in animal or demon form.","A member of one's family or household."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/familiar"},{"term":"beige","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A colour, variously defined from a pale brown, to a yellow greyish off-white.","Debeige; a kind of woollen or mixed dress goods."]},{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Having a slightly yellowish gray colour, as that of unbleached wool.","Comfortably dull and unadventurous, in a way that suggests middle-class suburbia."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/beige"}],"entries":[{"title":"Familiar","blocks":[{"text":"In European folklore of the medieval and early modern periods, familiars were believed to be supernatural entities, interdimensional beings, or spiritual guardians that would protect or assist witches and cunning folk in their practice of magic, divination, and spiritual insight. According to records of the time, those alleging to have had contact with familiar spirits reported that they could manifest as numerous forms, usually as an animal, but sometimes as a human or humanoid figure, and were described as \"clearly defined, three-dimensional... forms, vivid with colour and animated with movement and sound\", as opposed to descriptions of ghosts with their \"smoky, undefined form[s]\"."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/f0/Witches%27Familiars1579.jpg/330px-Witches%27Familiars1579.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Familiar","wikidata":"Q386905"},{"title":"Beige","blocks":[{"text":"Beige is variously described as a pale sandy fawn color, a grayish tan, a light-grayish yellowish brown, or a pale to grayish yellow. It takes its name from French, where the word originally meant natural wool that has been neither bleached nor dyed, hence also the color of natural wool."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/a5/Saint_Anthony_church_in_Chamboret_07.jpg/330px-Saint_Anthony_church_in_Chamboret_07.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Beige","wikidata":"Q843607"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/35/Histoires_prodigieuses%3B_Histoire_d%27un_prestre..._WMS_136_Wellcome_L0025557.jpg/500px-Histoires_prodigieuses%3B_Histoire_d%27un_prestre..._WMS_136_Wellcome_L0025557.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/35/Histoires_prodigieuses%3B_Histoire_d%27un_prestre..._WMS_136_Wellcome_L0025557.jpg/500px-Histoires_prodigieuses%3B_Histoire_d%27un_prestre..._WMS_136_Wellcome_L0025557.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"A story of \"a priest who for the space of 40 years employed a familiar spirit\", illustrated in Elizabeth I of England's copy of the Histoires Prodigieuses by Pierre Boaistuau","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AHistoires_prodigieuses%3B_Histoire_d'un_prestre..._WMS_136_Wellcome_L0025557.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/5b/Matthewhopkins.png/330px-Matthewhopkins.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/5b/Matthewhopkins.png/330px-Matthewhopkins.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Frontispiece from the witch hunter Matthew Hopkins' The Discovery of Witches (1647), showing witches identifying their familiar spirits","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AMatthewhopkins.p